<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #eaf2f8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Study Habits for Success</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How I Learned to Focus</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Assist in negotiations between student groups. Concept: Interpret graphical data for classroom use.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can assist groups in negotiating study-related tasks by rephrasing ideas simply (e.g., asks, "Can we all agree on study times?" while showing two options: "morning or afternoon" on the board). Can interpret basic charts or schedules related to study habits by highlighting key times (e.g., explaining a sample study schedule, pointing to "3-4 p.m." for after-school study time). Can clarify group tasks by explaining simple data from a graph, like a bar chart on "study hours per week" (e.g., "More time in English can help.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Display a simple, maybe slightly ambiguous, bar chart showing "Student Study Preferences" (e.g., "Preferred Study Time: Morning vs. Afternoon" or "Solo vs. Group Study"). Ask students to discuss in pairs: "What do you think this graph shows? How would you explain it to someone who finds it confusing?" This activates prior knowledge about study habits and introduces the concept of clarifying information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to interpret and mediate the displayed chart. "This chart <b>suggests</b> that many students <b>prefer</b> afternoon study, but some also find morning effective. What this means is, we <b>might</b> need to offer options. I <b>have developed</b> a routine to focus better in the mornings, but I <b>used to procrastinate</b> a lot. What have you done to change your study routine?" The teacher explicitly uses target grammar (present perfect, past simple) and vocabulary, focusing on the pronunciation of /st/ in "suggests" and the /d/ sound in "developed".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I ask you to explain the chart, am I asking you to just read the numbers, or to tell us what the numbers mean for our study habits?" (Expected: "To tell us what they mean and help us understand.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If two group members have different study suggestions, what is your role as a mediator? Do you choose one side or help them agree?" (Expected: "Help them agree by clarifying and rephrasing their ideas.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Study Habits for Success"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A digital infographic or a printed bar chart titled "Impact of Consistent Study Routines on Grades" showing two groups: "Students with inconsistent habits" vs. "Students with consistent habits" and their average grades. Alongside, a short text describing the benefits of "effective study strategies" and how "frequent practice" leads to "improvement" and "achievements."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will interpret and explain the relationship between study consistency and academic results from graphical data, facilitating peer understanding.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main message this infographic is trying to convey about study habits and grades?" (e.g., "Consistent habits lead to better grades.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match phrases like "low grades" to "inconsistent habits" and "high achievements" to "organized routines" based on the graph and text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>WH-questions:</b> "What impact have consistent study routines had on student grades according to the chart?" "How can a student develop more effective strategies?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice mediating discussions and interpreting data using present perfect for achievements ("I have developed better habits"), past simple for reflecting ("I used to procrastinate"), WH-questions ("What have you done to improve...?"), and key vocabulary (manage, organize, effective, progress, goals, strategies, discuss, suggest, clarify). Focus on clear pronunciation of /sk/ (skill), /st/ (strategy), and -ed endings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Cloze for Clarification:</b> Provide sentences or short dialogues related to study habits and group discussions with blanks. Students complete them using appropriate mediation phrases or target vocabulary. Example: "Student A: I want to study <b>every day</b>. Student B: But I only want to study <b>sometimes</b>. Mediator: So, <b>can we agree on</b> a schedule where we study <b>frequently</b> but also take breaks? What have you done to improve your focus?" (Words: suggest, clarify, agree, rephrase, negotiate). Practice pronouncing words like "accomplished" (/t/) and "suggested" (/ɪd/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled – Data Interpretation Prompts:</b> Present students with simplified data scenarios (e.g., a mini bar chart showing two students' "study hours per week" for English and Math). In pairs, Student A describes the data, and Student B asks clarifying WH-questions ("What do these results suggest?", "How has this student managed their time?"). Then, they swap roles. They should focus on using phrases like "This graph shows...", "What I understand is...", "It seems that...", "More time in English can help achieve better grades."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Rephrasing Ideas:</b> Give pairs a list of slightly complex statements about study habits (e.g., "Procrastination significantly impedes academic progress"). One student reads a statement, and the other must rephrase it in a simpler, clearer way, as if helping a classmate understand. (e.g., "So, what you mean is, if you delay your study, your grades will suffer."). Encourage using vocabulary like 'improve', 'develop', 'achieve', 'effective', 'essential'.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Study Habits for Success"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: "Study Habit Tracker Group Negotiation"</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in groups of 3-4. Each student brings their draft "Digital Study Habit Tracker" (from the 21st Century Skills Project, potentially with hypothetical data like "I <b>have frequently</b> reviewed my notes," or "I <b>used to</b> get distracted").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 1: Tracker Presentation & Interpretation:</b> Each student presents their tracker, explaining its features and interpreting the (hypothetical) data or progress shown. They should describe how they <b>have developed</b> certain habits or how they <b>used to procrastinate</b> and what strategies they <b>have implemented</b> to improve. They use frequency adverbs to describe their progress.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 2: Group Study Strategy Negotiation:</b> The group's task is to negotiate and agree on a collaborative study plan for an upcoming mock exam or project, integrating insights from their individual trackers. Students must use mediation skills to:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Explain</b> their own preferences and challenges based on their tracker data ("My tracker <b>suggests</b> I need more time for Math").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Rephrase</b> ideas if a group member doesn't understand another's suggestion ("So, <b>what I hear you saying is</b> we should prioritize group discussions <b>after</b> 4 p.m. because that's when you have more focus?").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarify</b> potential misunderstandings ("Can we all <b>agree on</b> these study times? What about a 10-minute break <b>every 40 minutes</b>?").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Assist in negotiations</b> by presenting options, summarizing arguments, and encouraging consensus ("This graph shows X has achieved great results with daily reviews, so perhaps we <b>could</b> incorporate that?").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They aim to create a shared study schedule (e.g., "Let's create a study schedule where we review one subject per day and take 10-minute breaks after 40 minutes of studying. We should use the Pomodoro technique, where we study for 25 minutes, then take a 5-minute break. This helps us stay focused without feeling overwhelmed.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation using a rubric and peer assessment. The teacher uses a checklist to evaluate individual and group mediation skills during the negotiation and presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their negotiated study strategy and shared schedule to the class. One or two designated "mediators" from the group will explain how they reached consensus, how individual habit tracker data informed their decisions, and how they resolved any disagreements. They must interpret their "Study Habit Tracker" data to justify their chosen strategies (e.g., "Based on our trackers, we've seen that consistent effort <b>has led</b> to improved grades, so we've decided to <b>always</b> review notes <b>before</b> class.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mediation Effectiveness:</b> Ability to rephrase ideas, clarify tasks, and facilitate negotiation towards a group consensus.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Data Interpretation:</b> Accurate and clear explanation of graphical data from personal habit trackers or general study charts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Linguistic Accuracy & Fluency:</b> Consistent and appropriate use of present perfect (achievements), past simple (past habits), WH-questions, and target vocabulary (manage, prioritize, effective, progress, goals, strategies, discuss, suggest, clarify).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Intelligible pronunciation, especially of contrastive sounds (/sk/, /st/) and -ed endings (/d/, /t/, /ɪd/), and stress on main verbs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Demonstrates active listening and respect for diverse ideas within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did I help my group understand complex ideas or data from our habit trackers during our negotiation?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one specific mediation strategy I learned today that I can use in other group projects?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Considering the 'Study Habit Tracker' project and our group negotiation, what progress have I made in improving my own study routines, and how can I inspire others with my achievements?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How have I used my English skills to clarify, explain, and facilitate understanding among my peers about effective study strategies?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students effectively mediate discussions and interpret data from their habit trackers? Were they able to rephrase ideas simply and assist in negotiating group tasks? Which groups demonstrated strong mediation skills, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Were the target grammar structures (present perfect, past simple) and vocabulary adequately integrated into their mediation tasks? Was the pronunciation practice on /sk/, /st/, and -ed endings evident in their speech?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or additional scaffolding might be needed to further develop their mediation skills, particularly in interpreting more complex data or facilitating deeper negotiations in future projects?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "How I Learned to Focus" by integrating all skills and culminating in a practical application of mediation through the "Study Habit Tracker" project. The communication, collaboration, and critical thinking skills developed here are transferable and essential for future academic success and real-world problem-solving in any subject or context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>